# TypeScript — Type-System Standards


Builds on `220-javascript.mdc` (modules, async, errors, security, testing) — both apply to `.ts` files.

## Compiler Configuration


- TypeScript version is authoritative from `package.json`; type-check with `tsc --noEmit`
  (`vue-tsc --noEmit` for Vue) — must pass in the `make check` gate.

```jsonc
// tsconfig.json — baseline; never weaken these flags to silence an error
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "es2023",
    "module": "nodenext",               // Node.js; bundler apps: "preserve" + "moduleResolution": "bundler"
    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,   // arr[i] is T | undefined
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,       // forces `import type`
    "erasableSyntaxOnly": true,         // no enum/namespace/parameter properties
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true
  }
}
```

- `// @ts-ignore` is forbidden; `// @ts-expect-error <reason>` only with a reason and a
  linked issue.
- Lint with **typescript-eslint** `strictTypeChecked` + `stylisticTypeChecked` presets.

## No `any`, No Lies


```ts
// ❌ any disables checking; `as` and `!` assert without proof
function parse(raw: any) { return raw as User; }
const name = users.find((u) => u.id === id)!.name;

// ✅ unknown + narrowing; absence handled explicitly
function isUser(value: unknown): value is User {
  return typeof value === 'object' && value !== null && 'id' in value;
}
const user = users.find((u) => u.id === id);
if (user === undefined) throw new NotFoundError(`User not found: id=${id}`);
```

- **No `any`** (explicit or implicit) — use `unknown` and narrow. Third-party `any` is
  wrapped and typed at the import boundary.
- No non-null assertions `!`; `as` casts only right after runtime validation or for `as const`.
- Prefer `satisfies` over annotation when you need both checking and the narrow inferred type.
- Avoid `Function`, `Object`, `{}`, `object` — write the real shape or signature.
- Explicit return types on exported functions (public contract, faster type-checking).

## Modelling Types


```ts
// ❌ enum — not erasable, emits runtime code, numeric enums accept any number
enum Status { Draft, Published }

// ✅ const object + derived union type
export const STATUS = { draft: 'draft', published: 'published' } as const;
export type Status = (typeof STATUS)[keyof typeof STATUS];

// ✅ Discriminated union + exhaustive check
type FetchResult =
  | { kind: 'ok'; data: Document }
  | { kind: 'notFound' }
  | { kind: 'error'; error: Error };

function describe(result: FetchResult): string {
  switch (result.kind) {
    case 'ok': return result.data.title;
    case 'notFound': return 'Not found';
    case 'error': return result.error.message;
    default: {
      const unreachable: never = result;   // compile error when a variant is added
      throw new Error(`Unhandled result: ${JSON.stringify(unreachable)}`);
    }
  }
}
```

- `interface` for object contracts that are implemented or extended; `type` for unions,
  intersections, mapped and utility types.
- Model states as **discriminated unions**, not as bags of optional flags
  (`isLoading?`, `error?`, `data?`) — make impossible states unrepresentable.
- **Immutability**: `readonly` properties, `ReadonlyArray<T>` / `readonly T[]` parameters,
  `as const` for literal tables.
- **Branded types** for IDs and validated values that must not be mixed up:

```ts
type UserId = string & { readonly __brand: 'UserId' };
type OrderId = string & { readonly __brand: 'OrderId' };
declare function cancelOrder(orderId: OrderId, by: UserId): Promise<void>;  // swapped args = compile error
```

- Generics: constrain them (`<T extends Entity>`), name them meaningfully when more than
  one (`TKey`, `TValue`); no generic that is used only once.

## Validation at Boundaries


Static types vanish at runtime. Everything entering the program (HTTP bodies, API
responses, `process.env`, files, `localStorage`, `postMessage`) is `unknown` until a
schema validated it. Derive the type **from** the schema — one source of truth.

```ts
import { z } from 'zod';

export const EnvSchema = z.object({
  DATABASE_URL: z.url(),
  PORT: z.coerce.number().int().positive().default(3000),
});
export type Env = z.infer<typeof EnvSchema>;

export const env: Env = EnvSchema.parse(process.env);   // fail fast at startup
```

## Errors


- `catch (error)` is `unknown` (`useUnknownInCatchVariables` via `strict`) — narrow with
  `instanceof` before use; never annotate it as `any`.
- Error subclasses declare `override name`; expected, recoverable failures in domain
  logic may be returned as a discriminated union (`Result`-style) instead of thrown.

```ts
export class NotFoundError extends Error {
  override name = 'NotFoundError';
}
```

## Classes and Dependency Injection


- Depend on interfaces, inject via constructor (DIP); explicit fields instead of parameter
  properties (`erasableSyntaxOnly`); `#private` for runtime privacy.

```ts
// Pattern: Repository + Dependency Injection (DIP)
export interface DocumentRepository {
  findById(id: DocumentId): Promise<Document | undefined>;
}

export class DocumentService {
  readonly #repo: DocumentRepository;

  constructor(repo: DocumentRepository) {
    this.#repo = repo;   // injected — replaced by an in-memory fake in tests
  }
}
```

## Imports and Documentation


- Type-only imports use `import type { User } from './user.js'` (`verbatimModuleSyntax`).
- Path aliases (`@/…`) must be configured identically in `tsconfig.json` and the bundler/test runner.
- TSDoc on every export: semantics only (`@param name - meaning`, `@returns`, `@throws`) —
  never repeat types already in the signature.
